---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="UK Tax Codes Explained: 1257L, BR, D0, K and More (2026/27) | wagecheck"
  description="A complete guide to UK tax codes for 2026/27. Learn what 1257L, BR, D0, K codes mean, how to check yours, and what to do if it's wrong."
  breadcrumbName="Tax Codes Guide"
>
  <section class="bg-gradient-to-br from-emerald-50 via-white to-lime-50">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 lg:py-20">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.24em] text-emerald-700">Guide</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl">
        UK Tax Codes Explained: <span class="font-display italic text-emerald-600">1257L, BR, D0, K</span> and More
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-600">
        Your tax code determines how much income tax HMRC takes from your pay. This guide explains every common UK tax code for the 2026/27 tax year, how to read yours, and what to do if it looks wrong.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500">Last updated: September 2026 · Tax year 2026/27</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none space-y-8 text-base leading-7 text-slate-700">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">What is a tax code?</h2>
        <p>
          A tax code is a combination of numbers and letters that tells your employer how much income tax to deduct from your wages before you receive your payslip. HMRC issues tax codes based on your Personal Allowance — the amount you can earn each year before paying any income tax.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the 2026/27 tax year the standard Personal Allowance is £12,570. This means you can earn up to £12,570 without paying income tax. Your tax code reflects this allowance so that your employer deducts the correct amount each pay period.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tax codes are not permanent. HMRC updates them when your circumstances change — for example, if you start a new job, receive benefits from your employer, or have underpaid tax in a previous year. Checking your tax code regularly ensures you pay the right amount and avoid surprises on your payslip.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Common tax codes explained</h2>
        <p>The table below covers the most common UK tax codes for 2026/27 and what each one means:</p>

        <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 text-left text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500">
                <th class="px-4 py-3">Tax Code</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3">Meaning</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3">Who gets it</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">1257L</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Standard Personal Allowance of £12,570</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Most UK employees — the default code</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">BR</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">All income taxed at 20% (basic rate)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Second jobs or where all PA is used elsewhere</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">D0</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">All income taxed at 40% (higher rate)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Second jobs where income exceeds £50,270</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">D1</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">All income taxed at 45% (additional rate)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Income above £125,140 on a second job</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">NT</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">No tax deducted</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Where no tax is due (e.g. very low pay)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">K prefix</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Negative allowance — tax added to income</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Benefits in kind exceeding allowance</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">W1 / M1</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Emergency tax code — week 1 or month 1 basis</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">New jobs where HMRC hasn't issued a code yet</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">S1257L</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Scottish version of 1257L</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Scottish taxpayers (different income tax rates)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">How to read your tax code</h3>
        <p>
          The number in your tax code is your Personal Allowance divided by 100. So 1257 means £12,570. The letter tells HMRC how to apply that allowance. "L" means you get the standard allowance. "T" means your code includes other calculations. "BR" means all income is taxed at the basic rate with no allowance applied.
        </p>
        <p>
          If your tax code has a suffix like "W1" or "M1", it is an emergency code. This means your employer calculates tax on a week-by-week or month-by-month basis rather than cumulatively. Emergency codes are usually corrected within a few pay cycles once HMRC processes your information.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">How to check your tax code</h2>
        <p>You can find your tax code in several places:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Your payslip</strong> — most employers print the tax code at the top or in the deductions section.</li>
          <li><strong>Your P45</strong> — when you leave a job, the P45 shows the tax code you were on at that point.</li>
          <li><strong>HMRC Personal Tax Account</strong> — log in at <a href="https://www.gov.uk/log-in-register-hmrc-online-services" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-700 font-semibold">GOV.UK</a> to see your current tax code and update your details.</li>
          <li><strong>Your employer's payroll department</strong> — they receive tax code notifications from HMRC.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If you have multiple jobs, you will have a different tax code for each one. Your main job usually gets the standard 1257L code, while secondary jobs are typically coded BR, D0 or D1 depending on the expected income.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">What to do if your tax code is wrong</h2>
        <p>
          If your tax code looks incorrect, contact HMRC directly. You can call the Income Tax helpline on 0300 200 3300, or update your details through your Personal Tax Account online. Common reasons for incorrect codes include:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>You started a new job and HMRC hasn't issued an updated code yet.</li>
          <li>You have benefits from your employer (company car, private health insurance) that reduce your Personal Allowance.</li>
          <li>You owe tax from a previous year and HMRC is collecting it through your code.</li>
          <li>You have multiple jobs and the allowances haven't been split correctly.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          HMRC will recalculate your code and send a revised notice to you and your employer. If you have underpaid or overpaid tax, HMRC will adjust future deductions or issue a refund.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Tax code changes in 2026/27</h2>
        <p>
          For the 2026/27 tax year, the Personal Allowance remains frozen at £12,570. This has been the case since 2021 and is currently planned to remain frozen until 2028. The standard tax code is therefore 1257L for most employees.
        </p>
        <p>
          Scotland continues to use its own income tax bands with rates of 19%, 20%, 21%, 42%, 45% and 48%. Scottish taxpayers receive codes prefixed with "S" (such as S1257L) but the Personal Allowance amount is the same.
        </p>
        <p>
          The National Insurance threshold aligns with the Personal Allowance at £12,570, meaning you start paying NI at the same point you start paying income tax. For 2026/27, employee NI is charged at 8% between £12,570 and £50,270, then 2% above that.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-2xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 p-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Tax Code Calculator</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
          Want to see how your tax code affects your take-home pay? Our <a href="/salary-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-700">salary calculator</a> lets you enter your salary and see exactly how much you take home after income tax, National Insurance, pension and student loan deductions. You can also try our <a href="/scottish-salary-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-700">Scottish salary calculator</a> if you pay Scottish rates.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Frequently asked questions</h2>

        <div class="mt-4 space-y-4">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">What does the L mean in 1257L?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">"L" means you are entitled to the standard Personal Allowance. It is the most common suffix for UK tax codes.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Why am I on an emergency tax code?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">Emergency codes (ending in W1 or M1) are temporary. They are issued when HMRC hasn't received enough information to issue a full code — for example, when you start a new job. Your employer should receive an updated code within a few weeks.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900">Can I change my tax code?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">You cannot change it yourself, but you can contact HMRC to correct it if you believe it is wrong. Update your details through your Personal Tax Account or call HMRC on 0300 200 3300.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
